{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfwL1QhZmexRa7Ui868jj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. Its status is still \"pending\" because it hasn't run yet. I didn't check that the rule's 5xx query matches any documents, and I haven't opened the dashboard to confirm the panels render.\n\n**Data:** The sample logs have these fields:\n- `timestamp`\n- `response` (a string with a `.keyword` subfield)\n- `bytes`\n- `extension`\n- `clientip`\n- `geo.src` and `geo.dest`\n- `machine.os`\n- `host`\n- `url`\n- `request`\n- `agent`\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-2251c441 Web logs overview\" has four panels:\n- Total requests, as a single number.\n- Requests over time, as a line chart.\n- Requests by response code, as a pie chart.\n- Average bytes by file extension, as a bar chart.\n\nIt is at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-2251c441-web-logs-overview-255e7b3349a4.\n\n**Rule:** \"EFF-2251c441 5xx spike\" (id `acb290e8-71c2-4335-8770-99aaabe08c98`) runs every minute. It is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It looks at the last 5 minutes and fires when more than 10 documents match a regex on `response.keyword` (`5[0-9][0-9]`). I used the regex because `response` is a string field, so a range query wouldn't work on it.\n\nThe sample data's timestamps are shifted to look recent when the data is installed, so the rule may not see any current 5xx traffic."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1204,"cache_read_input_tokens":19740,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1204},"output_tokens":540,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a4eb1cb7-866c-5bbf-8d81-a8d5a